== Result
<result>
+ #strong[Item 2: weights do not rescue the glued arm G.] In Singular
  4.3.2 `std` at m \= 4, t \= 4, F finished in 105 s with standard
  grading (`dp`) and in 97 s with its natural weights (eᵢ ↦ i). G did
  not finish within 1,800 s under either `dp` or its natural weights (s
  ↦ 1, q ↦ 2). So G/F is above 17 under `dp` and above 18 under weights,
  both lower bounds. At the cut-off G\'s weighted degree (37) already
  exceeded F\'s entire run (34). G\'s round-3 handicap is not just a
  grading artefact, at least within this budget. Singular is about 10×
  slower than msolve on F (105 s against 10.7 s), so only the ratios
  matter.

+ #strong[Item 4: the swap-symmetry split is real but buys nothing in
  msolve.] Exchanging the two cherries is a symmetry of the m \= 4 tree.
  In the coordinates a \= v₀ + v₁, b \= v₀ − v₁, all 19 generators are
  even or odd in the 9 b-variables (10 even, 9 odd, none mixed);
  `parity.sing` checks this.

  - msolve on the adapted system gives the same D \= 7 and the same
    quotient dimension (6), in 680 s against T\'s 697 s. Its widest
    matrix is 3.7× wider (94,422 against 25,742 columns), because the
    new coordinates make the polynomials denser.
  - An engine that used the even/odd block split could save at most
    about 2^{ω−1}, roughly 2–3.5×, in linear algebra. That would first
    have to pay back the 3.7× fill. It is a constant factor in any case.

+ #strong[Item 3: the cost law puts the crossover with rho far beyond
  cryptographic sizes] (`CROSSOVER.md`). The model takes the measured
  degree laws (D \= 4 + 1.5(m − 2), plus β(t − 4) with β ∈ {0, 0.5})
  into the dense Macaulay cost ω·log₂ C(N + D, D).

  - #strong[Asymptotically] the model is subexponential: the ratio to √p
    falls without bound.
  - #strong[At 256 bits] even the most optimistic row (ω \= 2, β \= 0)
    costs 2^{156} against rho\'s 2^{128}. With the measured depth slope
    it is 2^{298}–2^{399}.
  - #strong[The crossover] lies roughly between 2^{500} and 2^{6000}.

+ #strong[Item 1: the big-host run is blocked, and its specification is
  frozen] (`BIGHOST.md`). Two things block it:

  - This container\'s AWS keys are rejected by STS
    (`InvalidClientTokenId`).
  - The portable `isolab.eks.request/v1` contract runs only ecbench
    specs of at most 3,600 s, while this workload is msolve. It needs
    the EKS MCP\'s `isolab_k8s_run` or an ecbench extension.

  Inputs, resources, stop conditions and the outcome table are all
  fixed. None of its outcomes changes the 256-bit comparison in item 3.

#strong[Reading.] All four items point the same way. The tree encoding
is the only one of the measured encodings that scales in arity (rounds
2–3), and round 4 finds no encoding trick that lowers its cost: no
weighted grading, no elimination, no symmetry split. Its implied cost
law could be subexponential, but it would beat rho only far beyond
cryptographic sizes. That is the measured distance remaining for this
line. Closing it needs a PDP solver whose cost is not the dense Macaulay
size, which is a different research direction from factor-base symmetry.

