At m ≥ 5, F\'s input degree alone (16, 32) exceeds T\'s measured D (≥ 9,
≥ 10) already. So for m ≥ 5 the tree is the only encoding left, unless a
weighted grading rehabilitates the symmetric ones (item 2,
`WEIGHTED.md`).

== 2. Model
<2-model>
Factor base V \= g·μ\_{2^t}, with about 2^{t−1} usable points; arity m;
p ≈ 2^{mt} (the balanced base, one decomposition per target on average).
Per relation, with log₂ throughout:

- PDP solve ≈ ω · log₂ C(N + D, D), with N ≈ m(t + 1) unknowns (the
  measured N is 4t + 2 at m \= 4, 5t + 3 at m \= 5);
- targets per relation ≈ m!, with the symmetry quotient charged as in
  the lab\'s convention;
- relations ≈ |F| ≈ 2^{t−1};
- linear algebra ≈ 2(t − 1), from a sparse |F|² solve.

The degree law has two parts, and both are extrapolated:

- #strong[Arity:] D \= 4 + 1.5(m − 2). This is the round-3 lower-bound
  slope; the measured points are 4, 6, 7 at m \= 2, 3, 4.
- #strong[Depth:] + β(t − 4). Here β \= 0 is the optimistic case. β ≈
  0.5 matches T\'s staircase at m \= 3 (6, 6, ≥ 7 at t \= 4, 5, 6).

The total is the larger of (relations × PDP) and linear algebra,
minimized over m. \"Ratio\" below is log₂ cost / log₂ √p, so a ratio
below 1 beats Pollard rho.

