At ω \= 2.8, β \= 0 the optimum runs m \= 4 at 256 bits (cost 2^190.8
against rho\'s 2^128), m \= 6 at 1,024 bits and m \= 10 at 4,096 bits;
full table in §4.

- #strong[Asymptotically], under these laws, the ratio falls without
  bound, so the model is subexponential, as THEORY.md §3 predicted for D
  \= O(m). This holds only if the laws extrapolate. They rest on lower
  bounds at m \= 5, 6 and on t ≤ 6.
- #strong[At cryptographic size it does not come close.] At 2^256, even
  the most optimistic row (ω \= 2, β \= 0) costs 2^{156} against rho\'s
  2^{128}. With the measured depth slope it is 2^{298}–2^{399}. The
  crossover with rho lies roughly between 2^{500} and 2^{6000},
  depending on the row (interpolated between the table columns).
- #strong[What would change this:] a D law with a slope well below 1 per
  summand and β \= 0. Or a PDP solver whose cost is not governed by the
  dense Macaulay size C(N+D, D), for example one that exploits the
  tree\'s sparsity or treewidth. The lab\'s SAT/treewidth leads found no
  exponent movement
  (`research/ic_prime_eth_leads_20261031/SYNTHESIS.md`).

#strong[Consequence for the other items.] Confirming P2 on a big host
(item 1) can only make the asymptotic claim stronger; it cannot close
the gap at 256 bits. A swap-symmetry block split (item 4) is worth at
most 2^{ω−1}, under 2 bits, against a gap of at least 28 bits even in
the best row.

== 4. Commands
<4-commands>
The best-m search with ω \= 2.8 and β \= 0:

```sh
awk 'function lf(n,  s,i){s=0; for(i=2;i<=n;i++)s+=log(i); return s/log(2)} function lb(n,k){return lf(n)-lf(k)-lf(n-k)}
BEGIN{ for (bits=256; bits<=65536; bits*=2) { best=1e12;
  for (m=2;m<=400;m++){ t=int(bits/m+0.999); if(t<2)continue; D=int(4+1.5*(m-2)+0.5); N=m*(t+1); c=(t-1)+lf(m)+2.8*lb(N+D,D); la=2*(t-1); c2=(la>c)?la:c;
    if(c2<best){best=c2;bm=m;bt=t;bD=D}}
  printf "2^%-6d best m=%-3d t=%-5d D=%-4d log2 cost=%8.1f  log2 rho=%8.1f  ratio=%.3f\n", bits,bm,bt,bD,best,bits/2, best/(bits/2) } }'
```

